@rem Exercises for lesson 01_intro (C track). Format: docs/CONTENT_FORMAT.md

@exercise c-intro-theory-modulo
@type theory
@lesson 01_intro
@difficulty 1
@prompt
Which arithmetic operator gives the remainder of an integer division? Write the operator symbol.
@answer
% | the % operator | modulo | modulus
@hint
It is not / and it is not *.
@explain
The % operator returns the remainder: 10 % 3 is 1. It works on integers only.

@exercise c-intro-choice-intdiv
@type choice
@lesson 01_intro
@difficulty 1
@prompt
What does printf("%d", 7 / 2); print?
@choices
- 3.5
* 3
- 4
- 3.0
@explain
Both operands are int, so this is integer division and the fraction is thrown away: 7 / 2 is 3.

@exercise c-intro-fill-loop
@type fill
@lesson 01_intro
@difficulty 1
@prompt
Complete the loop so that it prints 0 1 2 3 4.
@code
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    for (int i = 0; ___; i++) {
        printf("%d ", i);
    }
    return 0;
}
@answer
i < 5 | i <= 4 | i != 5
@match loose
@hint
The loop must stop once i reaches 5.
@explain
i < 5 is true for 0, 1, 2, 3 and 4, so five numbers are printed.

@exercise c-intro-predict-divmod
@type predict
@lesson 01_intro
@difficulty 1
@prompt
What does this program print?
@code
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int a = 10, b = 3;
    printf("%d %d\n", a / b, a % b);
    return 0;
}
@answer
3 1
@explain
10 / 3 is 3 (integer division) and 10 % 3 is the remainder 1.

@exercise c-intro-bug-scanf
@type bug
@lesson 01_intro
@difficulty 1
@prompt
This program should read an age and print it. Click the line that contains the bug, then type the corrected line.
@code
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int age;
    printf("Enter age: ");
    scanf("%d", age);
    printf("You are %d\n", age);
    return 0;
}
@bugline 6
@answer
scanf("%d", &age); | scanf("%d",&age);
@match loose
@explain
scanf needs the address of the variable, so write &age. Without the & it treats the value of age as an address and crashes.
